#[doc("""
What guarantees no kalku outlives the run that summoned it.

A line at the end of a function runs on one path: the one where nothing
went wrong. A run that is cancelled, or unwound by a handler further out,
skips it and leaves children alive in the user's project — which Tier 1
says must never happen.

So summoning is watched and banishing is released, and the release lives
in the handler's `finally`, which runs on **every** path out of the
scope. A kalku banished in an orderly way is released and not killed
twice; anything still watched when the scope ends is killed there.

`finally` does not run on `panic`, which aborts the process rather than
unwinding it. Surviving that is the reni's job, not this one's.
""")]

import kalku.orchestrator.kalku_proc

#[doc("Keeps the summoned kalku of a run, so the run cannot end without them.")]
pub effect Ward {
  watch(k: Kalku) : Unit
  release(k: Kalku) : Unit
}

#[doc("Every watched kalku except this one, compared by the life it is living.")]
pub fn without(watched: [Kalku], k: Kalku) : [Kalku] =
  filter(watched, other => other.life != k.life)

#[doc("""
Run `body` with a ward: whatever it summoned and did not banish is
killed when the scope ends, however it ends.
""")]
pub fn warded[a, e](
  body: () -> a / Ward + Process + Telling + Clock + e
) : a / Process + Telling + Clock + e = handle {
  body()
} with Ward {
  initially {
    []
  }
  finally {
    each(state, k => kill_now(k))
  }
  watch(k, resume) -> resume((), [k, ...state])
  release(k, resume) -> resume((), without(state, k))
  return(x) -> x
}
